A||||8||88formula||Fuel injector sizing and finding the BSFC. HELP!||||1||
Z||000000||88formula||12-24-2000||01:40 PM||||I am calculating the injector size for my 3.4-liter engine using the formula (HP*BSFC)/(number of injectors * .8)= fuel injector size in lb/hr. I assumed the Brake Specific Fuel Consumption was .5 to get a fuel injector size of 21.7 lb/hr when I use that formula.<P>My question is how do I find the actual BSFC of my engine so I know that is accurate?<P>Thanks<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||12-24-2000||08:47 PM||||You have to find someone that has measured it and GM certainly wouldn't cough up any information more that .45-.55. Dyno the engine and get the data.<P>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-25-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||12-25-2000||12:51 AM||||If ahhhhhh anyone can answerrrrr my ahhhhhhh question than feel freeeeeeeeee to drop a post ahhhhh.<P>Back to the top.              dude<P><BR>ahhhhhhh thank you drive throughhhhhh.<P>dude||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||12-25-2000||02:36 AM||||Measure the HP and the fuel consumption at that HP. The BFSC is calculated from that.<P>HP * lbs/hr = BSFC.<P>Unless you or someone has jumped on a dyno and measure the HP and fuel consuption, you're stuck with .5 as the accepted average (which you know) for NA.<P>So, BFSC is great for ball parking the required flow rate, but adjusting the injector PW is the only way to fine tune the fueling....which led to my original question....<P>Now back to the program already in progress....<BR><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||12-25-2000||10:30 AM||||Back to the top.<P>My question is how do I find the actual BSFC of my engine so I know that is accurate?<P>Thanks<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||12-25-2000||06:56 PM||||To find BSFC, you have to put the engine on a dyno, and measure the fuel consumption while the engine is putting out a known amount of power. From there you can compute BSFC.<P>     What terryk is saying, and I agree, is that even with an accurate BSFC, which won't be very far from 0.5, you can only get close with injector sizing. If you REALLY want to get it RIGHT, then you are going to have to start reprogramming the chip.<BR>     Check out <A HREF="http://www.diy-efi.org" TARGET=_blank>http://www.diy-efi.org</A>  for more info.<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 12-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||88formula||12-25-2000||07:42 PM||||OK thanks for the info Will. I remember reading about that somewhere. After reading through that web-site I think I will just leave the custom prom up to a professional. <P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||12-25-2000||08:07 PM||||What injectors were you planning to use?||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||12-26-2000||05:23 PM||||I was planning on using either the original 2.8 injectors or the 3.4 injectors that came with my motor. The 2.8 injectors are professionally blueprinted and balanced by Marren Motor Sports. The 3.4 injectors are untouched with about 80,000 plus miles on them. I would like to use the 2.8 injectors if I can. If I cannot use them than I will have to sell them.<P>They are going into a 3.4-liter engine with 9.7-1 compression, ported heads, and headers, which should make about 210 horsepower according to Dyno2000. I am still working on the motor so it will be awhile before I get to actually installing the injectors. I will have to find someone to make me a chip after I get this thing together.<BR>||||reg||1
